Title: roar close-and-clean: a repeated --remove-worktree is silently dropped
Created: 2026-11-09
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-11-09T11:07Z claude created

--- body ---
## Problem
Observed in sen-causal-reading on 2026-11-09, roar step 10.

A session that owned three merged worktrees ran one `after-exit` call naming
all three, following step 10's "For a task with multiple completed PRs,
repeat the options":

    close-and-clean.sh --phase after-exit \
      --remove-worktree <A> --remove-worktree <B> --remove-worktree <C> \
      --local-branch a=<oid> --remote-branch a=<oid> ... (four pairs)

Only `<C>`, the last path, was removed. `<A>` and `<B>` stayed registered and
locked, their branches were then reported `RETAINED (occupied worktree)`, and
the run ended `RESULT: findings (exit 1): SWEEP`, which does not say that two
named worktrees were ignored. The session found out only by listing
`git worktree list` afterwards; a second and third call, one tree each,
cleaned up.

Mechanics (`skills/roar/close-and-clean.sh`): `--local-branch` and
`--remote-branch` append to arrays (l. 40), `--remove-worktree` assigns a
scalar (l. 47, `RM_TREE="$2"`), so the last value wins with no message. The
usage line lists all three flags alike, and step 10's wording ("repeat the
options") sits next to the `--remove-worktree` example, so nothing tells the
caller that one of the options does not repeat.

## Why it matters
A named cleanup target dropped without a word is an all-clear that cannot be
told apart from a real one: the caller believes the trees are gone, locked
worktrees accumulate, and their branches are kept as "occupied". Every roar
covering more than one worktree (several PRs in one session is common) is
exposed.

## Open design
Not prescribed: reimagine the fix. Candidate directions seen so far,
none chosen: accept several worktrees; refuse a repeated flag with a usage
error; one call per tree, documented in step 10 and the usage line. Weigh
them against how step 9's exit check prints the `after-exit` command for a
single tree, and against the ownership rule (only trees this task owns).

## Exit criteria
- [ ] No call naming several worktrees ends with any of them silently ignored
- [ ] Step 10 and the script's usage text agree on how several trees are handled
- [ ] A test pins the chosen behaviour for two `--remove-worktree` targets
